d then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535627 false] [17592186334589 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535627 true] [17592186334589 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535627 false] [17592186358199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358200 13194139535627 true] [17592186358199 1089 "q-152255" 13194139535627 true] [17592186358199 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535627 true] [17592186358199 1100 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535627 true] [17592186358199 1113 #uuid "9aeeaa0f-307c-589b-84c4-fc6bc50fe634" 13194139535627 true] [17592186358199 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/ff41c6fa5858f45e76a4d4fec0f0a8080300b69fcb632df60141d93c66f3cdd9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535627 true] [17592186358200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358201 13194139535627 true] [17592186358200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358202 131941395356